  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Data Sufficiency for Age Problems",
    "description": "Decide which age clues are enough for one unique answer, and which statements are redundant or still insufficient.",
    "teachingApproach": "Use short, connected phone-sized teaching screens with a concrete age situation before the equation, label every past/present/future date clearly, show the reason for each age shift, and check the result in the original clue. After each worked example, give a fresh near-transfer question. Wrong answers receive a misconception-specific correction and a parallel retry.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 32,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 8, Problems on Ages. Approved scope: Teach the chapter's data-sufficiency approach for age problems. Emphasize that the task is to decide whether the requested age or ratio is uniquely determined, not necessarily to carry every calculation to the end. Test Statement I alone and Statement II alone before combining them; distinguish I-alone, II-alone, either-alone, both-together, and even-both-insufficient cases. Then extend to three-statement questions by testing statement pairs and identifying redundant information. Use age totals, gaps, ratios, direct ages, and simple dated relations from the chapter. Never borrow information from another statement while testing one alone."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Data sufficiency asks a different question: not 'what is the age?' but 'do I have enough information to find it uniquely?' You can stop as soon as uniqueness is proved.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Question: What is A’s age?\nStatement I: A+B=30.\n\nThis is not enough. Many pairs total 30.\n\nSufficient means one unique answer, not merely a useful equation.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Test each statement on its own first. Ask: can more than one valid age still fit? If yes, that statement alone is insufficient.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
